{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4a1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07450-f188-71b1-8e87-3b58e1805190"},"sequence":171,"recorded_at":1780531400000337,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"acdfe288-4f4e-444a-ae51-26b969af2141","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"acdfe288-4f4e-444a-ae51-26b969af2141","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"acdfe288-4f4e-444a-ae51-26b969af2141"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"742f41cb-9e92-42cc-aa7d-86d018056818"},"task_id":"742f41cb-9e92-42cc-aa7d-86d018056818","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"742f41cb-9e92-42cc-aa7d-86d018056818","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2z3se0/TASK-260830-2z3se0_review-verdict-rev2.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2z3se0 — reviewer verdict, CR-TASK-260830-2z3se0-2 revision 2\n2|\n3|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`\n4|- repeat-of: `rev1/F1` and `rev1/F2` **by class, not by site** — both rev1 findings are themselves closed; two new findings reproduce their shape at different checks. Detail under *Routing*.\n5|- Reviewed tree: candidate `4291b80f56f30f399ace7b79a7b189e139ff0582` (base `1cb6b93585749df4ef4755ce93e486bcde9e3a6b`), `repository_delta=present`, 29 paths.\n6|- Working-tree OID recomputed against the candidate before the first mutant and after every batch; unchanged at the end (`TREE-OID-OK`, `git status --short` identical to the handoff).\n7|\n8|## Baseline reproduced by this run\n9|\n10|| Check | Result |\n11|| --- | --- |\n12|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n13|| `go vet ./internal/sessadapter/` | exit 0 |\n14|| `gofmt -l internal/sessadapter/` | empty |\n15|| `go test ./... -count=1` | 17/17 packages ok |\n16|| `go test ./internal/sessadapter/ -cover` | 81.4% of statements |\n17|\n18|## G-D — traversal ratio\n19|\n20|Gates were discovered by traversing production source, not by following tests:\n21|every `range` loop over a member list, every package-level vocabulary composite\n22|(61, enumerated by parsing production independently of the census), every\n23|equality chain against a host fact, every bound guard. Each gate was attacked by\n24|keeping it present and weakening it to admit exactly one member of the class it\n25|must reject; deletions were used only where the reject class has one member.\n26|Every mutant was grep-confirmed present and `go vet`-clean before its verdict.\n27|\n28|**121 narrowing mutants over 93 production gate sites: 110 killed, 11 survived (91%).**\n29|Round 1 was 63 of 87 over 45 sites (72%). The two are not the same measurement —\n30|the traversal widened as well as improved, chiefly because the 61 vocabulary\n31|tables are now enumerated exhaustively rather than sampled.\n32|\n33|Reported separately, outside the ratio: 11 census shape probes (below), 3\n34|controls (all behaved as required), 2 open-class mutants that survive by the\n35|recorded F8 bound, 1 equivalent mutant excluded (`secondLength < 0` is subsumed\n36|by the following range test), and 2 behavioural bypass probes.\n37|\n38|**Round-1 survivors: 22 of 24 closed. Zero resurrections.** The two remaining are\n39|the `unknownMember`/duplicate-member open-class pair, which the inventory header\n40|now records as an explicit stated bound — correct, and not a finding. Every\n41|round-1 kill I re-measured stayed dead (12 of 12; the 61-table battery re-covers\n42|every table round 1 sampled).\n43|\n44|| rev1 finding | Probe this round | Result |\n45|| --- | --- | --- |\n46|| F1 census blind to non-table vocabularies | named-type + inline-chain shapes, and all 61 tables widened | closed (S2, S3, 57/61 killed) |\n47|| F2 `var x = ctor` alias hole | 3 alias spellings + struct-field + slice-literal + method-value | closed, 6 of 6 |\n48|| F3 surrogate pairing bound | both edges, both directions, plus the first-surrogate top edge | closed, 5 of 5 |\n49|| F4 set-valued gates on a sample | 16 obligation-loop mutants, grow and shrink | closed, 16 of 16 |\n50|| F5 same-length context forgery | length-only and 32-byte-prefix narrowings | closed, 2 of 2 |\n51|| F6 8 MiB edge | both directions on all three entry points | closed, 6 of 6 |\n52|| F7 zero-value host facts | the four named sites | closed, 4 of 4 — but see N4 |\n53|| F8 open-class member gates | 2 exemption mutants | survives by design; bound recorded in the inventory header |\n54|\n55|## G-A — obligation sets are derived, and guarded in both directions\n56|\n57|The question was whether the obligation set is *derived* or merely enumerated\n58|more fully. Answer: **derived where it can be, and fail-closed where it is\n59|enumerated.** 16 of 16 mutants killed, including the \"fifth capability tomorrow\"\n60|case the brief named.\n61|\n62|| Gate | Denominator from production | Obligations driven | Grow probe | Shrink probe |\n63|| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n64|| `CheckDoctorHealthy` usable loop | `DoctorRequiredCapabilities` × 2 writer variants = 4 + 4 | 8 of 8 | A1 KILLED (count pin + `TestDoctorRequiredCapabilities`) | A2 KILLED |\n65|| `CheckDoctorHealthy` name loop | same | 8 of 8 | — | A3 KILLED |\n66|| `checkValidateNullability` | inline `targets`, 4 × 2 branches | 8 of 8 | A4 KILLED (archive positive case) | A5, A6 KILLED |\n67|| `checkValidateResult` applicable | inline, 3 | 3 of 3 | — | A7 KILLED |\n68|| `checkValidateResult` nullable-bool | inline, 3 | 3 of 3 | — | A8 KILLED |\n69|| `CheckTargetWriteGates` adapter | inline, 3 + the 2-member pair | 5 of 5 | A10 KILLED (`TestTargetWriteComplementDerivesNonRequired`) | A9 KILLED |\n70|| `CheckTargetWriteGates` provider | inline, 2 | 2 of 2 (× 2 failure modes each) | — | A11 KILLED |\n71|| `decodeCapabilities` | `capabilityOrder`, 15 | 15 of 15 | — | A12 KILLED |\n72|| `missingMember` | per-body `*Required` | — | — | A13 KILLED |\n73|| `requestMemberSet` / `successMemberSet` | `requestBodyMembers` / `successBodyMembers` | — | — | A14, A15 KILLED |\n74|| `validOperation` | `operationOrder` | — | — | A16 KILLED |\n75|\n76|**Obligations driven over obligations that exist: 52 of 52.** Where the list is\n77|written inline rather than read from a helper (`targets`, the two validate\n78|triples, the two target-write lists), the addition direction is still closed —\n79|by the archive positive case and by the derived non-required complement — so a\n80|fifth member added tomorrow reddens rather than shipping unguarded. That is the\n81|substantive answer to G-A, and it is a real improvement over \"four of four\n82|hand-written\".\n83|\n84|## G-B — the census after F1: 5 of 11 vocabulary shapes seen\n85|\n86|A four-member closed vocabulary was planted and consulted live from\n87|`CapabilityUsable` (behaviour-neutral: the planted set equals `capabilityStatuses`,\n88|which the value is already validated against upstream), once per shape. The\n89|denominator was built by enumerating how a closed set can be written in Go,\n90|independently of the census.\n91|\n92|| Shape | Census |\n93|| --- | --- |\n94|| package-level slice composite (control) | **seen** |\n95|| named slice type composite | **seen** — F1's C2 closed |\n96|| inline `\\|\\|` comparison chain | **seen** — F1's C3 closed |\n97|| package-level `map[string]struct{}` | **seen** |\n98|| tagged switch on a non-`operation` identifier | **seen** |\n99|| **tagless switch over the same members** | blind |\n100|| **function-local slice literal** | blind |\n101|| **`make()` + `init()` population** | blind (named in the stated bound) |\n102|| **`const` string + `strings.Contains`** | blind |\n103|| **`regexp.MustCompile` alternation** | blind |\n104|| **multi-name package-level `var a, b = []string{…}, []string{…}`** | blind |\n105|\n106|**Vocabularies found over vocabularies that exist: 5 of 11 shapes.** The extension\n107|is real and closes both shapes F1 named. Two of the six blind spots contradict\n108|the census's own text rather than its stated bound, which is finding N5.\n109|\n110|## Blocking findings\n111|\n112|### B1 (blocking) — an archive-mode validate success is unconditionally refused at the production entry point\n113|\n114|`checkValidateResult` (operations.go:1093) calls `checkValidateNullability` on the\n115|success body. The pinned §7.8 validate row gives the success body\n116|`{context, mode, valid, structural_valid, semantic_marker_valid, identity_valid,\n117|workspace_binding_valid, resume_surface_valid, findings, evidence_digest,\n118|extensions}` — no target members at all — while `checkValidateNullability` requires\n119|`projection_plan_id`, `projected_object_manifest_id`,\n120|`read_back_evidence_manifest_id`, `expected_target_native_session_id` to be `null`\n121|under `mode == \"archive\"`. `isNull(nil)` is `false` (decode.go:317: `\"\" != \"null\"`),\n122|so absent is not null and every archive success refuses.\n123|\n124|Driven at the production entry with the package's own fixtures — an archive\n125|request the package itself admits, then its success:\n126|\n127|```\n128|CheckSuccessBody(OpValidate, success, SuccessFacts{ValidateMode: \"archive\"})\n129|  → session_adapter_protocol_error: validate archive mode carries a target member\n130|    (member = projection_plan_id)\n131|```\n132|\n133|The refusal names a member the success body is structurally forbidden to carry.\n134|One of fourteen operations has one of its three modes unconditionally refused,\n135|and the AC row is \"Exact contract fixtures and negative/refusal cases pass\".\n136|\n137|The producer found this during F4, preserved behaviour, recorded it in\n138|`LOGBOOK.md`, and referred it for a product decision with three options. Finding\n139|it and not papering over it is the right call, and the honesty is noted. But it\n140|is **not** a product decision: the pinned document's success-body member list —\n141|the same list the member-set census already derives from that row — resolves it.\n142|Option (a), scope the rule to request bodies, is the only reading consistent with\n143|the pinned member table; (b) would break staged/live successes and (c) contradicts\n144|the row, which states `mode:staged|live|archive` on the success side too. This is\n145|rework inside the package, with no human input required.\n146|\n147|Whatever the fix, the success side needs a driving test: today no committed test\n148|constructs an archive-mode validate success, which is why 904 tests stay green\n149|over an operation mode that cannot succeed.\n150|\n151|### B2 (blocking) — four unknown-member gate maps carry no content pin, and one is a confirmed bypass\n152|\n153|`TestClosedMemberSetsAreDerivedFromSpec` compares both the ordered `*Required`\n154|list and the `*Members` map for its 17 `ordered` rows. The four bodies handled by\n155|the special-cased tail compare **only** `*Required`:\n156|\n157|| Map | Site | Widened by one name |\n158|| --- | --- | --- |\n159|| `successMembers` | protocol.go:370 | **SURVIVED** |\n160|| `failureMembers` | protocol.go:460 | **SURVIVED** |\n161|| `manifestMembers` | manifest.go:119 | **SURVIVED** |\n162|| `doctorResultMembers` | probe.go:584 | **SURVIVED** |\n163|\n164|`manifestMembers` additionally *looks* pinned: census_test.go:892 declares\n165|`manifestMembers := deriveManifestTableMembers(t, text)`, a local that **shadows the\n166|package-level map**, and then compares it against `manifestRequired`. The\n167|production map is never read in that function.\n168|\n169|No cardinality guard backstops any of the four — `len(members) != …` appears only\n170|in `decodeCapabilities` and one tuple site. Confirmed behaviourally, not inferred:\n171|with `successMembers` widened by one name, a frame carrying `ax.planted.member` is\n172|**ADMITTED** by `CheckSuccessEnvelope`; the unwidened control refuses it. The other\n173|three share the identical construction (`unknownMember(members, X)` with no\n174|cardinality guard); I confirmed one of the four behaviourally and report the other\n175|three as unpinned by identical shape rather than claiming four bypass probes.\n176|\n177|**16 of 20 unknown-member gate maps pinned.** The census reports 63 registrations\n178|and green, because a registration row proves the table is *known*, not that its\n179|contents are *pinned*. This is F1's class — a closed set the census reports as\n180|registered while nothing pins its content — at a different check and on the\n181|outermost gate in the package.\n182|\n183|Failure scenario: a later leaf adds a member to `successMembers` (or the other\n184|three) rather than to `successRequired`. Every test stays green, the census\n185|reports 63/63, the member-set census reports 20 closed bodies derived, and the\n186|success envelope admits a member the contract never defined.\n187|\n188|Two adjacent reporting defects at the same site: `t.Logf(\"member-set census: %d\n189|closed bodies derived\", len(ordered)+3)` reports 20 while 21 derivations run\n190|(17 ordered + success + failure + manifest + doctor); and one `ordered` row\n191|(`SupportedContractVersions`) carries `members: nil` by design. Neither number\n192|distinguishes 16-of-20 from 20-of-20 — the count needs to be the count of\n193|*member-map comparisons*, not of bodies.\n194|\n195|### B3 (blocking) — the inventory's constructor-closure sentence is false\n196|\n197|inventory_test.go:81 states: *\"refusalConstructors is the closed constructor set\n198|the derivation observes. A production refusal built any other way is invisible to\n199|the inventory **and fails the alias gate**.\"* The second clause does not hold.\n200|\n201|| Probe | Result |\n202|| --- | --- |\n203|| direct-call arm through `failInvalid`, **replacing** an existing arm (control) | KILLED |\n204|| additive arm through `failInvalid` (control) | KILLED |\n205|| package-level `var alias = failInvalid` | KILLED |\n206|| function-local `var alias = failInvalid` | KILLED |\n207|| `alias := failInvalid` | KILLED |\n208|| constructor in a struct field | KILLED |\n209|| constructor in a slice literal | KILLED |\n210|| method value calling `failInvalid` with non-literal args | KILLED |\n211|| **8th constructor `failQuota` + additive arm, direct call** | **SURVIVED** |\n212|| **inline `axerror.New(...)` refusal + additive arm, no constructor** | **SURVIVED** |\n213|\n214|The alias closure itself is now genuinely complete — all three spellings F2 named\n215|plus three more, and the header sentence describing it matches the walk exactly\n216|(\"exempts only the definition's own Name in its ValueSpec\"). That half of G-C is\n217|closed and should not be re-litigated.\n218|\n219|What is not closed is the layer above it. `refusalConstructors` is a hand-written\n220|list of seven with no production-derived denominator. Production currently imports\n221|`axerror` only in protocol.go with exactly seven `axerror.New` call sites — so the\n222|list happens to be complete today, and nothing enforces that. Both survivors ship\n223|an unwitnessed refusal arm through the full suite. The earlier probes were killed\n224|only because they *replaced* an existing arm and tripped the 323 floor; the\n225|additive form is the shape a producer would actually write.\n226|\n227|This is F2's class — a documented completeness guarantee ahead of the walk — in\n228|the same file, one sentence below the one that was fixed, and it undermines the\n229|323/323 figure that is this package's headline evidence.\n230|\n231|## Non-blocking findings\n232|\n233|### N4 — F7's class is unclosed at `CheckCallBinding`\n234|\n235|The four sites rev1 named are closed (F7a–F7d killed). The same shape at\n236|`CheckCallBinding` (discovery.go:196–229) is open: exempting the zero value of the\n237|compared binding fact survives on all five gates.\n238|\n239|| Gate | Zero-fact exemption |\n240|| --- | --- |\n241|| `role != binding.Role` | **SURVIVED** |\n242|| `context.ProviderID != binding.ProviderID` | **SURVIVED** |\n243|| `context.ManifestDigest != binding.AdapterManifestDigest` | **SURVIVED** |\n244|| `context.ExecutableSHA256 != binding.ExecutableSHA256` | **SURVIVED** |\n245|| `context.Environment != admitted` | **SURVIVED** |\n246|\n247|Baseline fails closed by accident of comparison against a zero struct, exactly as\n248|rev1 wrote of the other four. Either add the cases or record the bound explicitly;\n249|fixing four named instances of a class and leaving the fifth site unnamed is how\n250|the class comes back.\n251|\n252|### N5 — the census's stated scope is still ahead of its walk in two places\n253|\n254|The stated bound is honest about runtime-built sets and correctly names `init()`.\n255|Two other sentences are not bounds but claims, and both are false:\n256|\n257|- *\"scans every package-level var shaped like a vocabulary\"* — `vocabularyTablesInFile`\n258|  skips any `ValueSpec` with `len(value.Values) != 1`, so\n259|  `var a, b = []string{…}, []string{…}` is a package-level composite the scan never\n260|  sees.\n261|- *\"Any other switch is a membership gate hiding spot and fails\"* — the switch census\n262|  classifies **every** tagless switch as OK (`if statement.Tag == nil { classified++ }`).\n263|  The comment justifies this by naming the UTF-16 hex classifier; the code admits\n264|  the whole shape. Production has exactly one tagless switch today, so nothing sits\n265|  in it yet — but a tagless `switch { case v == \"a\": … }` is invisible to the table\n266|  scan, the chain scan and the switch scan at once.\n267|\n268|Two further blind spots are not named by the bound at all: a function-local\n269|composite (a shape production already writes at five sites — `targets`, the two\n270|validate triples, the two target-write lists) and a compile-time union encoded in a\n271|`const` string or a `regexp` alternation. Neither is \"built at runtime\".\n272|\n273|None of these is a live bypass today, which is why this is not blocking: every\n274|function-local set production currently holds is behaviourally pinned in both\n275|directions by the G-A battery. It is the claim, not the coverage, that is wrong.\n276|\n277|## What is correct and should not be re-litigated\n278|\n279|- **F1 through F7 are genuinely closed**, each verified by a mutant of my own\n280|  construction rather than by reading the rework note. 22 of 24 rev1 survivors dead,\n281|  zero resurrections, 72% → 91% on a wider traversal.\n282|- **F4's obligation sets are derived and two-sided** — the doctor set is read from\n283|  `DoctorRequiredCapabilities` with a count pin, and the target-write set has a\n284|  derived non-required complement. This is the right discipline and it answers G-A.\n285|- **The alias closure is complete and its sentence matches the walk.** Six spellings\n286|  attacked, six killed.\n287|- **F3's pair sweep is generated, not hand-written.** Both edges, both directions,\n288|  plus the first-surrogate top edge, all killed.\n289|- **F6 pins the bound on both sides at all three entry points.**\n290|- **F8 is correctly recorded as a stated bound** rather than faked with a\n291|  hand-picked witness.\n292|- **The durable-state bound is true, not asserted**: the package imports no `os`,\n293|  `net`, `exec`, or `time.Now` — the \"no crash/idempotency evidence applicable\" AC\n294|  row is verified, not taken on trust.\n295|- **The anomaly was surfaced, not buried.** B1 exists because the producer\n296|  reproduced it, wrote it down in the rework note and `LOGBOOK.md`, and refused to\n297|  redesign behaviour under a review finding. That is the correct instinct; only the\n298|  routing is wrong.\n299|\n300|## Evidence-quality note\n301|\n302|The rev2 battery (26 killed + 1 control-green / 27) is honest about what it\n303|measured and its method bounds are accurate. It is **finding-scoped**, not\n304|traversal-scoped: 27 mutants aimed at F1–F7. That is why 11 survivors sit outside\n305|it — 4 unknown-member maps, 5 `CheckCallBinding` facts, 2 constructor-closure\n306|holes, none of which any rev1 finding pointed at. The DoD row asks for a\n307|killed/measured ratio **over a traversal of production gate sites**; rev2 reports\n308|\"Survivors: none\", which is true of its 27 and not of the package.\n309|\n310|## Routing\n311|\n312|`repeat-of: rev1/F1, rev1/F2 — by class, at different sites; both rev1 findings closed.`\n313|\n314|B2 reproduces F1's class (a closed set the census reports as registered while\n315|nothing pins its content) at `TestClosedMemberSetsAreDerivedFromSpec` rather than\n316|at the table scan. B3 reproduces F2's class (a documented completeness guarantee\n317|ahead of the walk) one sentence below the one that was fixed. B1 is new.\n318|\n319|This is the second consecutive round in which an evidence instrument's own\n320|completeness claim was found ahead of what the instrument does, at three distinct\n321|sites. Per the routing rule, the next step on that class should be a gate rather\n322|than a third site-by-site pass: a check that every claim of the form \"every X is\n323|seen / any other X fails\" is either enforced or demoted to a stated bound —\n324|for instance, deriving `refusalConstructors` from production (`axerror.New` call\n325|sites outside the registered set fail), comparing every `*Members` map against its\n326|derived list with a count of comparisons rather than of bodies, and making the\n327|switch census name the classifier it exempts instead of exempting the shape.\n328|\n329|## Requested changes\n330|\n331|1. **B1** — scope `checkValidateNullability` to request bodies (or state why the\n332|   success-side call is correct against the pinned §7.8 success member list), and add\n333|   a committed test that drives an archive-mode validate success through\n334|   `CheckSuccessBody`.\n335|2. **B2** — compare `successMembers`, `failureMembers`, `manifestMembers` and\n336|   `doctorResultMembers` against their derived member lists; remove the local\n337|   `manifestMembers` shadow at census_test.go:892; report the member-set census as a\n338|   count of member-map comparisons, not of bodies.\n339|3. **B3** — derive the refusal-constructor denominator from production instead of\n340|   listing it, so an 8th constructor or a bare `axerror.New` refusal fails the\n341|   inventory; then make the header sentence say what the walk does.\n342|4. **N4** — close or explicitly bound the five `CheckCallBinding` zero-fact gates.\n343|5. **N5** — fix the two census sentences that are claims rather than bounds\n344|   (multi-name `ValueSpec`, tagless switch), and name the function-local and\n345|   encoded-union blind spots in the stated bound.\n346|6. **Evidence** — report the next battery as a ratio over a traversal of production\n347|   gate sites, not over the finding list.\n348|\n349|No stop-the-line boundary was found. B1 looked like one and is not: the pinned\n350|document resolves it without a human decision, and every other finding is ordinary\n351|rework inside this package."}}}
